WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:03.919
In the course of adapting their comic book
properties to film and TV, Marvel has made

00:00:03.919 --> 00:00:06.450
some pretty big changes to their classic characters.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:09.950
While some changes have been for the best,
others have risked losing the magic that made

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:11.710
those characters great in the first place.

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:15.130
Crack open a longbox and take a look at how
these Marvel characters should really look.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:17.590
Daredevil

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:21.940
Netflix's Daredevil series keeps to gritty,
street-level heroics instead of the usual

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:26.230
globe-trotting Avengers fare, so naturally,
producers were nervous about undermining this

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:29.300
realistic approach with simple red spandex.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:33.170
Daredevil spent most of the first season fighting
crime in cargo pants and a black handkerchief

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:35.370
mask, all without his famous double Ds.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:39.609
When he finally donned a superheroic costume,
the comics' vivid red color scheme skewed

00:00:39.609 --> 00:00:43.980
more maroon, and most notably, the traditional
skintight costume was ditched in favor of

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:46.679
reinforced leather, accented in black.

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:49.910
It's more armored than what we've seen in
the comic pages, probably because getting

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:52.549
hit by nunchucks and swords really hurts.

00:00:52.549 --> 00:00:55.979
And by the end of the first season, Daredevil
finally gets the bright idea to wear a horned

00:00:55.979 --> 00:00:58.010
helmet instead of, well, nothing.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:02.079
Let's not even touch this guy's ridiculous
first comic appearance...which pretty much

00:01:02.079 --> 00:01:05.120
proved he was totally blind.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.100
Elektra

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:10.970
The biggest problem with comic book Elektra
is that no self-respecting female warrior

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:14.230
would be caught dead fighting in a skintight
swimsuit in the real world.

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:18.360
And Elektra, for some reason, is usually drawn
covered in so many wafting scarves that she

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:20.330
looks like Steven Tyler's microphone stand.

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:22.960
She's the very definition of an impractically
dressed assassin.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.480
She never even has a place to put her sais.

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:27.920
Give the poor woman a holster or something,
at least.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.430
Jeans, a sleeveless shirt, and a mask to hide
her identity?

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:34.550
That's far more in line with practical assassin
gear, and live-action Elektra wears it well,

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:37.280
opting for the same real-world practicality
as Matt Murdock.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:42.400
Though we'll see how crazy things can get
if Elektra somehow returns for season three.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:43.710
Luke Cage

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:48.450
He may have never adopted the skintight getup
popular with other superheroes, but Luke Cage's

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:52.390
original outfit was still pretty goofy, featuring
a silver headband and a yellow blouse open

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:53.440
to the waist.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:57.250
While that look undoubtedly killed at Studio
54, it didn't outlast the '70s.

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:01.680
By 1992, it was considered so cringeworthy
that Luke was shown tearing it to pieces on

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:03.130
the cover of his own comic.

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:07.420
However, it does get a fun shout-out in the
TV series, which sees Luke grab a yellow shirt

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:11.250
to wear while still sporting the metallic
headpiece and cuffs from the experiment that

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:12.480
gave him superpowers.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:16.170
Live-action Luke Cage prefers darker clothes
and hoodies, which wasn't simply an aesthetic

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:17.170
choice.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:20.319
Star Mike Colter told MTV News that the dark
hoodie was...

00:02:20.319 --> 00:02:22.580
"...symbolic because of Trayvon Martin.

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:25.860
We talked about that specifically, what that
would mean to people and the feelings it would

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.109
evoke in viewers.

00:02:27.109 --> 00:02:31.060
[...] What this show says politically resonates
profoundly."

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:32.560
Kilgrave

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:36.659
The nemesis of Jessica Jones, the Purple Man
is arguably one of Marvel's most terrifying

00:02:36.659 --> 00:02:37.659
villains.

00:02:37.659 --> 00:02:41.670
Originally a spy named Zebediah Killgrave,
a chemical accident turned his skin purple

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:44.499
and gave him the ability to command complete
obedience.

00:02:44.499 --> 00:02:48.700
In Jessica Jones, Kilgrave keeps his mind
control powers and the suit, but loses the

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:50.810
comic book backstory and purple skin.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:54.900
His "Purple Man" nickname is never mentioned,
and even "Killgrave" turns out to be an alias

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:58.980
used to hide his sinister true identity: Kevin
Thompson.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:03.510
Without purple skin, Kevin looks like everyone
else, hiding his nightmarish powers in plain

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.120
sight, which is arguably much more terrifying.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.200
Plus, it's easier just to make him look like
a perfectly unremarkable evil British guy,

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.900
who kind of looks like he's a candidate for
being the next Doctor Who.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:15.930
The Punisher

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:19.790
It's kind of hard to mess up the Punisher
— just dress an angry guy in a black T-shirt

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:20.900
and hand him a gun.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:24.620
Luckily, Jon Bernthal not only looks the part,
but does a spectacular job of portraying a

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:28.269
damaged soldier mentally spiraling downward
in the wake of immense tragedy.

00:03:28.269 --> 00:03:31.829
Anybody in a skull shirt and leather can pass
for a decent Frank Castle, but to be able

00:03:31.829 --> 00:03:33.530
to make The Punisher believable?

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:35.189
That's much harder to pull off.

00:03:35.189 --> 00:03:39.880
Bernthal might not always sport the iconic
skull, but even without it, the guy is unmistakeable.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:42.480
"Now I can ask you face to face.

00:03:42.489 --> 00:03:44.150
Who was there that day?

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:46.150
Who killed my family?"

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:48.049
The Kingpin

00:03:48.049 --> 00:03:51.080
Kingpin's traditional purple pants and white
jacket never made it beyond a quick reference

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:55.670
in the first season of Daredevil, probably
because the outfit is completely ridiculous.

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:59.699
Comic book Kingpin's ascot also seems to be
an upper-crust fashion relic that didn't make

00:03:59.699 --> 00:04:00.699
it into the new millennium.

00:04:00.699 --> 00:04:04.420
In its place around Vincent D'Onofrio's neck
is…nothing.

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:05.420
Not even a tie.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:07.579
Instead, Wilson Fisk wears the finest suits.

00:04:07.579 --> 00:04:11.150
Then again, he could be wearing clown shoes,
boxer shorts, and a tank top, and he would

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:12.419
still look like Kingpin.

00:04:12.419 --> 00:04:15.409
There are few actors on Earth who'd look better
in the role.

00:04:15.409 --> 00:04:19.140
Netflix and Marvel must thank their lucky
stars everyday for the gift that is D'Onofrio.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:27.510
"And when one comes across someone with such
talent, with such a gift.

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:30.750
Well, you don't let that go to waste."

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:33.380
"No.

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:36.880
No, you don't."

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:38.090
The Owl

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:42.720
Leland Owlsley underwent the biggest comics-to-screen
change of any Daredevil character.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:46.440
In terms of personality, both are financiers
of criminal enterprises, but beyond that they

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.000
differ wildly.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.150
Netflix's Leland is a bespectacled and bow-tied
walking suit.

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:54.810
Comic book Leland, or the Owl as he's better
known, looks so much like Wolverine that it's

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:57.500
not difficult to mistake the two animal-themed
weirdos.

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:01.730
Older versions of The Owl look a lot more
whimsical, sporting a green, Sherlock Holmes-like

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:05.690
Ulster coat, and occasionally, he wore a big
ridiculous red bowtie around his neck that

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:08.720
made his head look like an extravagantly wrapped
Christmas gift.

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:13.030
It's also not clear if Daredevil's stuffy,
nerdy Owl had hollow bones, deadly talons,

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:17.430
or the ability to glide...but we're guessing
not.

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:18.430
Thanos

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:22.290
The evil alien overlord Thanos has already
made a big impact in the Marvel Cinematic

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:25.640
Universe, despite standing up for only about
30 seconds.

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:29.480
His love of sitting is very true to the comics,
where the character's inventively named "Space

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:31.930
Throne" can travel between dimensions.

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:35.940
Compared to the comics, Thanos' costume has
been updated to give him a more armored look,

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:38.030
replacing skin-tight space spandex.

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:41.340
The blue-and-gold color scheme remains, and
his helmet is now a separate piece instead

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:42.980
of being connected to the neck plate.

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:47.450
The character's traditional purple complexion
actually did make an appearance in The Avengers,

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:50.200
but was replaced with a milder blue by Guardians
of the Galaxy.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:53.650
Maybe he just needs to work on his space-tan.

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:55.340
Loki

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:59.190
Tom Hiddleston's Loki has mostly stuck close
to the source material, although he's yet

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:04.240
to match his comic equivalent's gleeful mischievousness,
like turning Thor into a frog, for example.

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:07.790
His costume remains fairly faithful to the
comics, and even his famous horned helmet

00:06:07.790 --> 00:06:09.160
makes a few appearances.

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:13.190
The main change is the same one most comics
adaptations make: switching Loki's classic

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:16.080
skintight catsuit for something a little less
revealing.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:20.690
The costume has also been toned down a little,
with darker greens and less vivid gold trimmings.

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:24.610
That said, Loki is a shapeshifter, spending
notable periods of the comic as a child, a

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:28.940
woman, a magical unicorn, and he can even
pop off his own head when he feels like it...so

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:32.390
it's hard to say for sure what the character
should really look like.Thanks a lot for being

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:35.230
totally unpredictable, Loki!

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:38.920
"No, thank you."

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:39.960
Thanks for watching!

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:42.600
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:45.930
And leave us a comment to let us know what
you think about the costumes of Marvel's on-screen

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:46.520
heroes...

